{\"response\":35,\"explanation\":\"In the sample (n=601), respondents with children (n=430) reported a slightly lower mean affair frequency than those without children (1.36 vs 1.69), and nearly identical rates of any affair (24.7% vs 25.7%). The mean difference is not statistically significant (Welch t-test p=0.298; Mann–Whitney p=0.622), and the difference in any-affair proportions is also not significant (two-proportion z-test p=0.783). Effect sizes are very small (Cohen’s d = -0.10; risk difference = -1.1 percentage points; relative risk ≈ 0.96). Overall, the evidence does not support a meaningful decrease in extramarital affairs associated with having children.\"}
